<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chinese Communist Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1921">1921</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Communist Party of China, CCP</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chinese Communist Party is the founding and ruling party of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>. It held its first congress in the French Concession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> in 1921 and, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a>, won the civil war against the Nationalists in 1949. It has governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> ever since and has close to 100 million members.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party.jpg" alt="Chinese Communist Party" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Converted from File:Flag of the Chinese Communist Party.svg by PhiLiP with Inkscape.Optimized version by Mike Rohsopht · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Danghui.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> and Marxist theory inspired the founding of the party. Chen Duxiu and Li Dazhao were among the first to support Leninism and world revolution in public, and both regarded the October Revolution as heralding a new era for oppressed countries. In 1921 they founded the party with the help of the Far Eastern Bureau of the Russian Communist Party and that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Communist International</a>. In its first years it was aligned with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a>. The rise of the Kuomintang's right wing under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a>, and massacres of tens of thousands of party members, led to a split and a prolonged civil war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On 15 July 1927 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuhan">Wuhan</a> government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> expelled all communists. The party answered by founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Workers' and Peasants' Red Army</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and a battalion under General Zhu De took the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanchang">Nanchang</a> on 1 August 1927 in the Nanchang uprising. During about ten years of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> became the most influential figure in the party, which built a strong base among the rural peasantry with its land reform policies.</p><p>Support continued to grow during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-sino-japanese-war">Second Sino-Japanese War</a>, and after the Japanese surrender in 1945 the party defeated the Nationalist government. Mao proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> before a large crowd at Tiananmen Square on 1 October 1949, and the remnants of the Nationalist government retreated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>.</p><p>Mao remained the most influential member until his death in 1976. Under him the party completed its land reform, launched five-year plans and split with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> he tried to purge the party of elements deemed capitalist or reactionary. After his death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hua-guofeng">Hua Guofeng</a> removed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gang-of-four">Gang of Four</a> from power, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a> won a power struggle with Hua to become paramount leader in 1978. Deng, with Hu Yaobang and Zhao Ziyang, led the policies of reform and opening up, and argued that a socialist state could use a market economy without itself being capitalist.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The party seeks officially to adapt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism–Leninism</a> to Chinese conditions, a process it calls socialism with Chinese characteristics. It is organised on democratic centralism, which allows open discussion of policy on condition of unity once a decision is made. Its highest body is the National Congress, convened every five years. When the Congress is not in session the Central Committee is the highest body, but because it usually meets only once a year, most duties lie with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a> and its Standing Committee. The party's leader holds the office of general secretary, and also chairs the Central Military Commission.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chinese Communist Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The People’s Republic of China was created by the <b>Chinese Communist Party</b>, founded in Shanghai in 1921.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On 4 May 1919 students in Beijing protested against the transfer of German rights in Shandong to Japan at the Paris Peace Conference, and the May Fourth Movement fed modern Chinese nationalism and, in 1921, the founding of the <b>Chinese Communist Party</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-china">Republic of China · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Chen Duxiu and Li Dazhao, leaders of the movement, were the most influential promoters of Marxism in China in these years and became founders of the <b>Chinese Communist Party</b> in 1921; others drawn to communist ideas included Chen Tanqiu and Zhou Enlai.</blockquote><a href="/movement/may-fourth-movement">May Fourth Movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Chinese Communist Party</b> proclaimed the People’s Republic of China on 1 October 1949, and communist parties came to rule North Korea, Vietnam, Cuba and Laos.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Chinese Communist Party</b> claimed that the revolution had achieved women’s liberation, and the Nordic countries developed a state feminism of their own.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/feminism">Feminism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 1950s until the late 1970s Maoism was the political and military ideology of the <b>Chinese Communist Party</b>, and its mass campaigns, among them the Anti-Rightist Campaign of 1957–1958, the Great Leap Forward and the Great Proletarian Cultural Revolution, put Mao’s theory of cultural revolution into practice.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/maoism">Maoism · In Power</a></li></ul></section>
